    clinical:
      "<b>Why this matters in Indian practice.</b><br>" +
      "<b>Ask what a captive carnivore is actually being fed, and ask specifically about bone.</b> Hand-reared tiger and leopard cubs, captive lions and rescued wild cats fed on boneless muscle meat develop nutritional secondary hyperparathyroidism, and the presentation is characteristic — <b>a well-grown, apparently thriving cub that becomes reluctant to move, cries when handled, sits crouched, and then develops bent limbs or a fracture from ordinary activity</b>. Because the animal looks well nourished, keepers are often disbelieving. Explain the ratio plainly: <b>meat contains about twenty times more phosphorus than calcium, and the animal needs slightly more calcium than phosphorus</b>, so the skeleton is being dissolved to keep the blood calcium normal. The correction is to <b>feed whole prey or carcass with bone, or to supplement calcium properly</b>.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Radiograph early if you can, and handle the animal gently.</b> A cub with metabolic bone disease has bones that fracture on ordinary handling, so restraint for examination or radiography must be careful. Look for <b>thin cortices, generalised loss of density, folding fractures and vertebral collapse</b>. Warn the owner or keeper that <b>deformities already present will not correct</b>, even though further damage can be prevented — this manages expectations and prevents the treatment being judged a failure.<br><br>" +
      "<b>In reptiles and birds, ask about ultraviolet light, not just diet.</b> This is a question that is almost never asked and frequently explains the case. <b>Ordinary window glass blocks UV-B</b>, so an animal kept indoors near a sunny window gets no usable ultraviolet and cannot make vitamin D3 whatever it is fed. Ask whether there is a UV lamp, how far the animal can get from it, and <b>how old the lamp is — UV output falls long before the lamp stops looking bright</b>, so a lamp more than six to twelve months old may be providing nothing. Advise unfiltered natural sunlight where the climate allows, which in most of India it does.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Plan every capture around preventing capture myopathy, because it cannot be treated well once established.</b> The measures that work are all preventive: <b>keep pursuit as short as possible, never capture in the heat of the day, use appropriate chemical immobilisation rather than prolonged physical restraint, keep the animal cool, blindfolded and quiet, minimise transport time and noise, and ensure vitamin E and selenium status is adequate before a planned operation.</b> Explain to forest staff that a long chase, however skilfully it ends, may kill the animal a week later.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Spin a blood sample when a captured animal passes dark urine.</b> This takes two minutes with a haematocrit tube and settles the diagnosis. <b>Clear plasma with dark urine means myoglobin — capture myopathy. Red or pink plasma means haemolysis — babesiosis or another haemolytic disease.</b> The two look similar in a recently captured animal and are managed quite differently, so the test is worth making a routine part of post-capture monitoring, along with serum CK and AST where available.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Supplement thiamine routinely in every fish-fed animal, before signs appear.</b> Otters, fish-eating birds and captive crocodiles fed frozen fish will eventually develop thiamine deficiency, because <b>freezing does not destroy thiaminase</b>. Do not wait for star-gazing to appear. If a fish-eating animal does present with the head retracted over its back, ataxia or convulsions, <b>give parenteral thiamine immediately — the response is dramatic and within hours, and it confirms the diagnosis</b>. A therapeutic trial is entirely justified before any laboratory result.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Slice the brain and use an ultraviolet lamp in a blind, head-pressing wild ruminant.</b> It costs nothing. <b>Bright blue-white autofluorescence of the cut cortical surface in a darkened room indicates polioencephalomalacia.</b> Because <b>lead poisoning is clinically indistinguishable</b>, submit liver and kidney for lead estimation at the same time, and in a wild animal remember that <b>rabies must also be excluded</b> — so handle the brain with gloves and submit material for fluorescent antibody testing as well.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Split the femur at every wildlife post-mortem and record the marrow.</b> This is the standard objective measure of nutritional status. <b>Firm, fatty, white marrow means adequate reserves; gelatinous, watery, pink-grey marrow means terminal, prolonged starvation</b>, because the marrow is the last fat depot to be mobilised. Assess the coronary groove and perirenal fat at the same time. This graded assessment is what allows a defensible opinion in a <b>wildlife die-off investigation, a range carrying-capacity assessment, or a cruelty or neglect enquiry</b>, and it should be recorded and photographed.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Do not stop at the parasite in a wildlife die-off.</b> When several free-ranging animals die during a drought and are found to carry heavy worm burdens or a bacterial infection, consider whether <b>nutritional failure of the range is the underlying cause and the parasite merely the proximate one</b>. Assess body condition and marrow fat across the affected animals. If they are starving, the recommendation must address <b>habitat, water, carrying capacity and population management</b>, because deworming a starving population achieves very little.",

